# {{lb|en|lutherie|bowmaking}} {{synonym of|en|adjuster}}.
# The least amount of care or interest; a [[whit]] or [[jot]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Sheridan Le Fanu|title=The House by the Churchyard|passage='She has heard from us this morning,' said Mr. Gamble, grinning on his watch, 'and she knows all by this time, and 'tisn't a '''button''' to her.'}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=Van Tassel Sutphen|title=In Jeopardy|passage=As to that I did not care a '''button''', but I had wanted to hear about Betty, and now her name was barely mentioned.}}
# {{lb|en|television}} The [[punchy]] or [[suspenseful]] line of [[dialogue]] that [[conclude]]s a [[scene]].
#: {{syn|en|blow}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=David Kukoff|title=Vault Guide to Television Writing Careers|page=77
|passage=One thing you definitely don't want to do is write past the '''button'''. For example, a scene's natural '''button''' might run something like this:<br>TONY: That kind of talk is exactly what I'm talking about.<br>Whereas an example of writing past the '''button''' would sound something like this:<br>TONY: That kind of talk is exactly what I'm talking about.<br>CARMELLA: Okay. 'Bye.<br>TONY: Bye.}}
# {{lb|en|comedy}} The final joke at the end of a comedic act (such as a sketch, set, or scene).
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=Scenes usually go out on a laugh line, a stinger or a '''button'''. End your script with a twist!|title=Animation Comedy and Gag Writing|author=Jean Ann Wright|date=November 8, 2002|journal=Animation World Network|url=https://www.awn.com/animationworld/animation-comedy-and-gag-writing}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=With our show, one thing we wanted to do was give our best effort to always put a '''button''' on the scene.|author=Daniel Schindel|title=3 Comedy Sketches that Changed Key and Peele's Lives|date=June 18, 2014|journal=Los Angeles Magazine|url=https://www.lamag.com/culturefiles/3-comedy-sketches-that-changed-key-and-peeles-lives/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220524162752/https://www.lamag.com/culturefiles/3-comedy-sketches-that-changed-key-and-peeles-lives/|archivedate=24 May 2022}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=Is there a best way to end a comedy sketch? Endings — or outs, or '''buttons''' as writers call them — are notoriously difficult to nail. The ideal ending needs to be satisfying and surprising while staying true to the comedic game that preceded it.|journal=The Washington Post|date=July 12, 2016|author=Jessica Goldstein|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/how-to-best-end-a-comedy-sketch-its-hard-to-go-wrong-with-gruesome-death/2016/07/12/e8a01360-290b-11e6-ae4a-3cdd5fe74204_story.html|title=How to best end a comedy sketch? It’s hard to go wrong with gruesome death|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250722182321/https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/how-to-best-end-a-comedy-sketch-its-hard-to-go-wrong-with-gruesome-death/2016/07/12/e8a01360-290b-11e6-ae4a-3cdd5fe74204_story.html|archivedate=22 July 2025}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[button man]]; a professional assassin.
#* '''1973''', Mario Puzo, Francis Ford Coppola, ''The Godfather Part II'' (screenplay, second draft)
#*: FREDO: Mikey, why would they ever hit poor old Frankie Five-Angels? I loved that ole sonuvabitch. I remember when he was just a ''''button''',' when we were kids.
# The final [[segment]] of a [[rattlesnake]]'s [[rattle]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Statistical Study of the Rattlesnakes|page=26|author=Laurence Monroe Klauber|year=1936|passage=Hardly a rattler is ever reported in the newspapers unless it is stated to have had "blank rattles and a '''button'''". But here '''button''' usually means the terminal lobe of the last rattle, even though the string may not be complete, the true '''button''' and additional rattles having been lost.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|Southern US}} A [[clove]] {{q|of garlic}}.
# {{lb|en|zoology}} Pedicle; the attachment point for [[antler]]s in [[cervid]]s.

