====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover or seal with a cap.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[award]] a cap as a mark of [[distinction]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[lie]] over or on top of something.
## {{lb|en|by extension|graffiti slang}} To paint over someone else's [[piece]] or [[tag]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[surpass]] or [[outdo]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set (or reach) an [[upper]] [[limit]] on something.
#: {{co|en|to '''cap''' wages}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 September 6|author=Philip Haigh|title=£30 billion plan to transform the rail network in Ireland|journal=RAIL|issue=991|page=25|text=It recalls the business case for Scotland's reopening of the Borders Railway to Tweedbank, that British Rail closed in 1969. The review says the business case for this was at best borderline, but goes on to say that the case greatly underestimated passenger demand and that the railway Scotland built has '''capped''' its capacity.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To [[conclude]]; to make something even more wonderful at the end.
#: {{ux|en|That really '''capped''' my day.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Mike Isaac|title=X CEO Linda Yaccarino Says She Is Leaving Elon Musk’s Platform|work=The New York Times|date=2025-07-09|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/09/technology/linda-yaccarino-x-steps-down.html|issn=0362-4331|author2=Kate Conger|passage=Ms. Yaccarino’s exit '''caps''' a tumultuous period at X, which was previously called Twitter and has been remade in Mr. Musk’s image since he bought the platform for $44 billion in 2022.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260113072816/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/09/technology/linda-yaccarino-x-steps-down.html|archivedate=13 January 2026}}
# {{senseid|en|cricket}} {{lb|en|transitive|cricket}} To select a player to play for a specified side.
# {{senseid|en|shoot}} {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[shoot]] (someone) with a [[firearm]].
#: {{syn|en|pop a cap in someone's ass<alt:pop a cap into>}}
#: {{ux|en|If he don’t get outta my hood, I’m gonna '''cap''' his ass.}}
#: {{ux|en|In a school shooting, where some kid '''caps''' a bunch of other kids, where did he get the weapon? From a family member, probably their gun cabinet.}}
# {{senseid|en|lie}} {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|originally|AAVE}} To [[lie]]; to tell a lie.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906|author=w:Alfred Henry Lewis|title=Confessions of a Detective|chapter=Confessions of a Detective|location=New York|publisher=A.S. Barnes & Company|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/confessionsadet00lewigoog/page/n53/|page=36|passage="How? Didn’t I '''cap''' for you, an’ square you with the examinin’ board? Didn’t I stake you to the three hundred dollars?"}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=Tomb of the Boom|text=It’s over for you '''capping'''-ass rappers—get out the game / You can fool the record labels, but not the streets, man|year=2003|author={{w|Big Boi|Antwan Patton}}; et al.|artist=w:OutKast|album={{w|Speakerboxxx/The Love Below|Speakerboxxx}}}}
# {{senseid|en|sports}} {{lb|en|transitive|sports}} To select to play for the national team.
#: {{ux|en|Peter Shilton is the most '''capped''' English footballer.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To salute by uncovering the head respectfully.
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Henry Esmond|I|I Go to Cambridge, and Do But Little Good There|231|Tom never miſsed a lecture, and '''capped''' the proctor with the profoundeſt of bows.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|author=William Hill Tucker|title=Eton Memories|page=128
