#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=Locksley Hall|page=92|passage=Locksley Hall, that in the distance overlooks the sandy tracts, / And the hollow ocean-ridges roaring into '''cataracts'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Froude England|volume=VI|chapter=Queen [[w:Lady Jane Grey|Jane]] and Queen [[w:Mary I of England|Mary]]|page=1|passage=[T]here came a storm such as no living Englishman remembered. The summer evening grew black as night. '''Cataracts''' of water flooded the houses in the city and turned the streets into rivers; {{...}}}}
