==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|bite}}
An alteration of the word {{m|en|bite}} so it would not be accidentally misspelled as {{m|en|bit|id=binary digit}}. {{coin|en|Werner Buchholz|in=1956|nobycat=1}}, during the early design phase for the {{w|IBM 7030 Stretch}} computer.<ref>{{cite-journal|year=1977|title=The Word “Byte” Comes Of Age|month=February|work=Byte Magazine|volume=2|number=2|page=144|author=Werner Buchholz|passage=A byte was described as consisting of any number of parallel bits from one to six. Thus a byte was assumed to have a length appropriate for the occasion.|url=https://archive.org/stream/byte-magazine-1977-02/1977_02_BYTE_02-02_Usable_Systems#page/n145/mode/1up}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web |title=Timeline Of The IBM Stretch/Harvest Era (1956–1961) |work=Computer History Museum |url=https://archive.computerhistory.org/resources/text/IBM/Stretch/102636400.txt |archiveurl=http://web.archive.org/web/20110611043241/https://archive.computerhistory.org/resources/text/IBM/Stretch/102636400.txt |archivedate=2011-06-11 }}</ref><ref>{{cite-web |title=Definition of 'Byte' |titleurl=http://catb.org/~esr/jargon/html/B/byte.html |work=The Jargon File |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20030701094801/http://catb.org/~esr/jargon/html/B/byte.html |archivedate=1 July 2003 |year=2003 |accessdate=23 March 2007 }}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/baɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-byte.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪt|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|bight|bite|by't}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|computing}} A short [[sequence]] of [[bit]]s (binary digits) that can be [[operated]] on as a [[unit]] by a [[computer]]; the smallest usable [[machine word]].
# {{senseid|en|Q8799}}{{lb|en|computing|most commonly}} A unit of computing storage equal to eight [[bit]]s, which can [[represent]] any of 256 distinct values.
#: {{syn|en|B|octet}}
#: {{ux|en|The word “hello” fits into five '''bytes''' of ASCII code.}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=1984
|author=Neil Ardley
|title=Sinclair {{w|ZX Spectrum+}} User Guide
|publisher=w:Dorling Kindersley
|isbn=0-86318-080-9
|pageurl=https://z00m.speccy.cz/files/ZXSpectrumPlusUserGuide.pdf#page=50
|chapter=The ZX Spectrum+ Memory Map
|chapter_series=Learn About Your ZX Spectrum+
|chapter_seriesvolume=3
|page=48
|passage=If you look at the photograph of the interior of the Spectrum{{nb...}}, you will see that there is one ROM chip, and 16 smaller RAM chips. These chips give the Spectrum its memory. The memory consists of 65536 storage units that each contain one '''byte''' (a number from 0 to 255).
}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Eric Johnson; Joshua Jones|title=A Developer's Guide to Data Modeling for SQL Server
|passage=With a varchar, the storage is always the actual number of characters you have stored plus 2 '''bytes'''. So a varchar(20) with a 5-character word will take up 7 '''bytes''', with the extra 2 '''bytes''' holding a size reference for SQL Server.}}

