===Etymology 1===
{{multiple image|perrow = 2|total_width=450
| image1 = Napping cat in a potted plant store in Taichung 02.jpg
| caption1 = The term's [[namesake]].
| image2 = Cat Nap, 2003, oil.jpg
| caption2 = An artistic depiction of a woman and her cat taking a '''catnap'''.
| image3 = Nap in the Country, Nap in Town MET DP871840.jpg
| caption3 = A historical illustration of [[countryfolk]] and [[townsfolk]] '''catnapping''' with their pets.
| image4 = Der Mittagsschlaf.jpg
| caption4 = A [[farrier]] taking a [[midday]] '''catnap'''.
}}

From {{com|en|cat|nap}}. Named in reference to the [[feline]] habit of taking multiple, brief, and light naps throughout the day to conserve energy, rather than sleeping for one long, uninterrupted block.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[brief]], [[light]] [[sleep]], usually during the [[daytime]]. {{defdate|from 1820s}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=19 August 1963|author=w:Charlotte Curtis|title=Newport Debut Revelry Carries Over to 2d Day|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1963/08/19/archives/newport-debut-revelry-carries-over-to-2d-day.html|passage=By 9 A.M., however, all but the most insomniac of the night owls were taking a '''catnap'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1965 |title=The New Yorker |author=|page=28 |ISBN=|passage=Besides, I reflected, Joe might easily have become a catnapper, as so many millions of other Americans had become since Sarah Perkins, during the campaign, had made it so widely known that although the incumbent President slept in a bed with the covers over him for three and sometimes four hours a night, she herself never slept at all except for the '''catnaps'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010  |title=Save Our Sleep: Toddler |author=Tizzie Hall |page= 117|ISBN=|passage=A toddler who is still having two day sleeps will generally have one good sleep and one '''catnap''', and a toddler who is around three years of age and getting close to dropping her day sleep will catnap.}}

