===Etymology===
[[File:Campanile De St Marc 002.jpg|thumb|''Campanile di San Marco'' or [[w:St Mark's Campanile|St. Mark’s Campanile]], the [[bell tower]] of [[w:St Mark's Basilica|St. Mark’s Basilica]] in [[Venice]], [[Italy]].]]

{{root|en|ine-pro|*kh₂em-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|campanile|t=bell tower, belfry}}, from {{m|it|campana|t=bell}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=26768|date=March 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> + {{m|it|-ile|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|noun}}s indicating locations that host animals or objects}}. {{m|it||Campana}} is derived from {{der|en|la-lat|-}} and {{der|en|la-med|campāna|t=large bell used in late classical or medieval church towers or steeples; tower for such a bell, belfry, campanile}}, and then either:

* traditionally regarded to be from {{der|en|la|Campāna|t=region of Campania, Italy}} (because bells were supposedly introduced in Christian services in {{w|Nola}}, a diocese of Campania, by [[w:Paulinus of Nola|Saint Paulinus]] ({{circa2|354|short=yes}} – 431), though the story has been discredited),<ref>{{cite-book|author=H. W. P[hillott]|entry=Paulinus (8), St.|editors=[[w:Henry Wace (priest)|Henry Wace]] and William C[oleman] Piercy|title=[[w:Dictionary of Christian Biography and Literature to the End of the Sixth Century|A Dictionary of Christian Biography and Literature to the End of the Sixth Century A.D., with an Account of the Principal Sects and Heresies]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]],{{nb...|Albemarle Street, W.}}|year=1911|page=814|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofchri00waceuoft/page/814/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=1079300106|passage=Dr. Adolf Buse, {{...}} shews that the use of bells in churches, an invention credited to him [St. Paulinus] by tradition, is not due to him, nor even to the town of Nola.}}</ref> from {{m|la|Campānus|t=relating to the region of Campania, Italy, or its inhabitants, Campanian}}, from {{m|la|campus|t=field, plain}} (from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kh₂em-|t=to bend, curve}}) + {{m|la|-ānus|pos=suffix meaning ‘of or pertaining to’ forming {{glossary|adjective}}s, which are sometimes used as nouns}}; or
* from {{der|en|grc|καπάνη|t=felt helmet}} (apparently because of the similarity in shape).

The {{glossary|plural}} form {{m|en|campanili}} is derived from {{der|en|it|campanili}}.

