===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[convoy#Noun|convoy]] or [[procession]] of [[traveller]]s, their [[cargo]] and [[vehicle#Noun|vehicles]], and any [[pack animal]]s, especially [[camel#Noun|camels]] [[cross#Verb|crossing]] a [[desert#Noun|desert]].
#: {{synonyms|en|camel train|caravanserai|convoy|pack train|wagon train}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846|author=w:Rufus B. Sage|title=s:Rocky Mountain Life|passage=To the left the '''caravan''' animals, securely picketed, at regular distances of some fifteen yards apart, occupied an area of several acres.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|title=s:The Man Who Would Be King|passage=“Would they could have foretold that my '''caravan''' would have been cut up by the Shinwaris almost within shadow of the Pass!” grunted the Eusufzai agent of a Rajputana trading-house whose goods had been feloniously diverted into the hands of other robbers just across the Border, and whose misfortunes were the laughing-stock of the bazar. “Ohé, priest, whence come you and whither do you go?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|editor=[[s:Author:W. B. Kimberly|W. B. Kimberly]]|title=[[s:History of West Australia/Chapter 21|History of West Australia]]|chapter=21|passage=Camel '''caravans''', and courageous teamsters opened regular carrying businesses between Southern Cross and Coolgardie, while coaches began to run over the desert.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 July 27|author=Sir Michael Holden|title=In praise of Crossrail 1... and in search of Crossrail 2|journal=RAIL|issue=962|page=33|text=The '''caravan''' of skilled and experienced designers, tunnellers and builders follows these projects around the world.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|Britain|New Zealand|South Africa}} A furnished vehicle [[tow]]ed behind a car, etc., and used as a dwelling when stationary.
#: {{syn|en|camper<q:US>|recreational vehicle|RV|trailer<id:caravan><q:US>|travel trailer}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1825|author=Royal Society|work=Philosophical transactions of the Royal society of London : giving some accompt of the present undertakings, studies, and labours of the ingenious in many considerable parts of the world.|volume=115|page=76|passage=An Italian woman, twenty years of age, {{...}} was travelling in a '''caravan''' with the baggage of the Duke of Wellington's army, in the middle of the night, in a violent storm, while she was fast asleep, a small monkey with a long chain upon the roof of the '''caravan''' took refuge in it {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Roger Cross; Avon Hudson|title=Beyond Belief: The British Bomb Tests: Australia's Veterans Speak Out|page=92|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VClIwLXF5WcC&pg=PA92|passage=The '''caravans''' were the demarcation between the non-radioactive areas and the radioactive areas. There were two main '''caravans''', one for people going into the forward area, and the other '''caravan''' was for people returning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Chris Cleave|title=Incendiary|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jA4lo0I8pMoC&pg=PT94&dq=%22caravan%22%7C%22caravans%22+-intitle:%22caravan%7Ccaravans%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=x5QeT-u0F6e6iQet6KnqDQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22caravan%22%7C%22caravans%22%20-intitle%3A%22caravan%7Ccaravans%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=unnumbered
|passage=The best thing about '''caravans''' is that they're always exactly the same, said Terence Butcher. You can tow your '''caravan''' to Brighton or Bournemouth or Bognor. Doesn′t make the blindest bit of difference. When you close the door behind you at the end of the day you′re home.}}
# {{cln|en|collective nouns}} {{lb|en|collective}} A {{l|en|group}} of {{l|en|camel}}s, especially when used as beasts of burden carrying people and merchandise.
#: {{hyper|en|coffle|herd}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Twice, however, we encountered a '''caravan '''of camels-about seventy strong in one instance, and about thirty in the other.|page=64|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Cradle_of_Mankind/vSQFIP37CWYC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22a+caravan+of+camels%22&pg=PA64&printsec=frontcover|publisher=A. and C. Black|title=The Cradle of Mankind: Life in Eastern Kurdistan|author=William Ainger Wigram, Sir Edgar Thomas Ainger Wigram|year=1914}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=It was just about noon, and the mirage in the middle of the day quickly swallows up even a caravan of camels on the horizon, or they get hidden in a dip of the plain, and ours were now out of sight.|page=300|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Bedouin_Tribes_of_the_Euphrates/KOtBAAAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22a+caravan+of+camels%22&pg=PA300&printsec=frontcover|publisher=Harper and Brothers|title=Bedouin Tribes of the Euphrates|author=Lady Anne Blunt, Wilfrid Scawen Blunt|year=1879}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=As I sauntered to-day down the Rue d'Isly, on evolutionary thoughts intent, I met a '''caravan''' of camels, in long single file, coming in from the desert with their bales of merchandise.|page=392|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Eclectic_Magazine_of_Foreign_Literat/-rImAQAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22a+caravan+of+camels%22&pg=PA392&printsec=frontcover|publisher=Leavitt, Trow & Company|journal=The Eclectic Magazine of Foreign Literature, Science, and Art|year=1888|author=George Fleming|article=On a Certain Deficiency in Women}}

